The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if_id = (status & 0xFFFF0000) >> 16;
port_priv = ethsw->ports[if_id];
Reason:
The `if_id` is extracted from the hardware status register without validation. Since `if_id` can be any 16-bit value (0–65535) but `ethsw->ports[]` is allocated with only `ethsw->sw_attr.num_ifs` elements, an out-of-bounds read occurs when `if_id` is equal to or greater than `num_ifs`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
+	if (if_id >= ethsw->sw_attr.num_ifs) {
+		dev_err(dev, "Invalid if_id %d in IRQ status\n", if_id);
+		goto out;
+	}
Reason:
The added bounds check ensures that `if_id` is within the valid range (0 to `num_ifs-1`) before indexing `ethsw->ports[]`. If `if_id` is out of bounds, an error is logged and the handler jumps to `out`, preventing the out-of-bounds read.

